      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "@{5fca5df4d58780532024a52b|full_name} I'm not a fan of this argument because it implies that a badly designed animator is what is stopping this system from being implemented, which is exactly the type of animator that I don't want running in the background.\n\nUsing a similar example, I could argue that turning off animations in safety settings achieves what you're describing: it creates a disparity between the proper state of the avatar and the actual state for others. However, you could say in this example, the user is conscious of the choice. I would respond by saying that many new users have no extensive knowledge of the safety system or that avatars can *look different* based on their safety settings.\n\nAnother similar example is late-joining. The same state errors would occur if someone joins late and catches an avatar in an awkward in-between state. When people do care they usually recognize it and ask the person to repeat/reset the animation; it is a non-issue, IMO.\n\nAn actual issue with this system is avatars with flashy spawn animations. Repeating them over and over could get pretty annoying pretty fast. But that seems more like an issue with the avatar being annoying than with the system not working properly. *Remember how avatars with spawning sound effects were pretty popular back then, but they stopped showing up as much when people started to block them?*\n\nOne question that I don't see many people ask regarding this issue is: *What if turning off animators doesn't even save that much performance?* I frequently hear that turning off animators would save a lot of performance, but no one references any tests or numbers. I assume it would because some animators can get pretty nasty, but what about the average animator? If it doesn't then we should get some official response backed up with numbers.\n\nI agree that if there is a way to pause an animator while maintaining its states, it should be implemented instead, as that would be the preferable solution. But, in the meantime, that shouldn't stop this band-aid from being an option while disclosing to users what it does.\n\nIn my opinion, hiding animators shouldn't even be an option, *it should be mandatory,* for everyone. That would set a precedent for animators to use better logic. But I definitely acknowledge that it is a controversial opinion. I think people should be informed that you can create/adapt animators to account for late-joining.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2022-08-17T11:08:49.243Z",
  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "I would like to have an option to enable animator hiding. Maybe even just in a json file.\nBig animators can bring a massive CPU cost with it, and i would really like to hide them.\nWhile I understand that exposing that to most users would probably cause a lot of bug reports, i would like to still have it as a config options, where users that turn it on will know that that will cause issues with some avatars using write defaults.\nPersonally i havent seen many problems with it in the beta and i would like to keep the performance benefit.",
